1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,876
The Science Behind Cannabis.

2
00:00:01,876 --> 00:00:03,784
Episode two: What are cannabinoids?

3
00:00:03,784 --> 00:00:08,035
In this episode we look at the molecules themselves: what they have in common,

4
00:00:08,035 --> 00:00:11,142
how the plant makes them, and how chemists classify them.

5
00:00:12,642 --> 00:00:17,746
The word cannabinoid originally described a group of compounds found only in the cannabis plant.

6
00:00:17,746 --> 00:00:22,212
Chemically they are terpenophenolic compounds: each molecule combines a phenol ring,

7
00:00:22,212 --> 00:00:26,200
like the ones found in many plant products, with a terpene-derived portion,

8
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:29,868
built from the same building blocks as the scents of pine and citrus.

9
00:00:29,868 --> 00:00:32,899
Most of them share a skeleton of twenty-one carbon atoms.

10
00:00:32,899 --> 00:00:38,428
More than one hundred different phytocannabinoids, meaning plant cannabinoids, have now been identified,

11
00:00:38,428 --> 00:00:42,416
and they differ from one another in small details of this common framework.

12
00:00:43,916 --> 00:00:50,905
Inside the plant, cannabinoids are made in tiny glandular structures called trichomes, mainly on the flowers.

13
00:00:50,905 --> 00:00:55,586
The biosynthesis starts with a molecule called cannabigerolic acid, CBGA.

14
00:00:55,586 --> 00:01:00,844
Enzymes then convert CBGA into different acidic cannabinoids: THCA, CBDA, or CBCA,

15
00:01:00,844 --> 00:01:03,794
depending on which enzyme the plant expresses.

16
00:01:03,794 --> 00:01:07,834
These acidic forms are what the living plant actually contains.

17
00:01:07,834 --> 00:01:11,681
When the plant material is heated or stored for a long time,

18
00:01:11,681 --> 00:01:18,222
the acids lose a carbon dioxide group, a process called decarboxylation, and become the neutral forms:

19
00:01:18,222 --> 00:01:19,312
THC, CBD and CBC.

20
00:01:20,812 --> 00:01:23,858
A handful of phytocannabinoids are studied most.

21
00:01:23,858 --> 00:01:31,473
Delta nine tetrahydrocannabinol, THC, is the principal psychoactive constituent and binds to both cannabinoid receptors.

22
00:01:31,473 --> 00:01:35,725
Cannabidiol, CBD, has a very similar formula but a different shape;

23
00:01:35,725 --> 00:01:41,563
it binds only weakly to the cannabinoid receptors and interacts with several other proteins.

24
00:01:41,563 --> 00:01:46,449
Cannabigerol, CBG, is the neutral form of the biosynthetic starting material.

25
00:01:46,449 --> 00:01:53,112
Cannabichromene, CBC, and tetrahydrocannabivarin, THCV, which has a shorter side chain, are also present.

26
00:01:53,112 --> 00:01:56,349
Cannabinol, CBN, is not made by the plant directly;

27
00:01:56,349 --> 00:01:59,141
it forms when THC is oxidised during ageing.

28
00:02:00,641 --> 00:02:05,027
Two properties of these molecules shaped the whole history of the field.

29
00:02:05,027 --> 00:02:07,098
First, they are highly lipophilic:

30
00:02:07,098 --> 00:02:10,631
they dissolve in fats and oils but hardly at all in water.

31
00:02:10,631 --> 00:02:13,433
This is why they accumulate in cell membranes,

32
00:02:13,433 --> 00:02:19,160
and it is also why early scientists assumed they might act on membranes in a non-specific way.

33
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:24,338
Second, THC is chiral: it exists as mirror-image forms, like a left and a right hand.

34
00:02:24,338 --> 00:02:26,592
The natural form is (−)-trans-Δ9-THC.

35
00:02:26,592 --> 00:02:28,785
Its mirror image is far less active.

36
00:02:28,785 --> 00:02:33,475
That difference was an early clue that the molecule must fit into a specific,

37
00:02:33,475 --> 00:02:35,425
shaped binding site, a receptor.

38
00:02:36,925 --> 00:02:39,975
Today the term cannabinoid covers three families.

39
00:02:39,975 --> 00:02:43,648
Phytocannabinoids are the plant molecules we have just met.

40
00:02:43,648 --> 00:02:49,313
Endocannabinoids are molecules made by the body itself, which we will meet in episode four;

41
00:02:49,313 --> 00:02:53,858
they look chemically quite different, but they act on the same receptors.

42
00:02:53,858 --> 00:02:57,842
Synthetic cannabinoids are compounds designed in the laboratory.

43
00:02:57,842 --> 00:03:03,258
Some of them, such as CP-55,940, WIN 55,212-2, and HU-210, were made as research tools,

44
00:03:03,258 --> 00:03:07,367
and they turned out to be essential for discovering the receptors,

45
00:03:07,367 --> 00:03:12,036
because they could be made radioactive and bind much more tightly than THC.

46
00:03:13,536 --> 00:03:17,389
The plant also contains molecules that are not cannabinoids at all.

47
00:03:17,389 --> 00:03:21,299
The most studied are terpenes, such as myrcene, limonene and pinene,

48
00:03:21,299 --> 00:03:25,439
which give cannabis its characteristic smell and are also found in hops,

49
00:03:25,439 --> 00:03:26,992
citrus peel and pine resin.

50
00:03:26,992 --> 00:03:31,707
Different plant varieties contain different mixtures of cannabinoids and terpenes,

51
00:03:31,707 --> 00:03:36,250
and scientists study how these mixtures differ from a single purified molecule.

52
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:42,921
The idea that minor components can modify the activity of a main component is sometimes called the entourage effect,

53
00:03:42,921 --> 00:03:45,509
a term we will return to in the last episode.

54
00:03:47,009 --> 00:03:52,561
In the next episode, we follow the experiments that proved cannabinoids act through specific receptors,

55
00:03:52,561 --> 00:03:55,257
and showed where in the brain those receptors are.
